1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,734
Welcome to the Modern Defence.

2
00:00:01,734 --> 00:00:03,713
The idea is simple, and a little sneaky.

3
00:00:03,713 --> 00:00:05,840
Black does not rush a pawn into the center.

4
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:08,165
Instead, the king's bishop slides onto the long

5
00:00:08,165 --> 00:00:10,491
diagonal behind a pawn, the knight and a modest

6
00:00:10,491 --> 00:00:12,667
center pawn follow, and Black castles early.

7
00:00:12,667 --> 00:00:15,636
White is invited to build a big pawn center, and that is the

8
00:00:15,636 --> 00:00:18,010
point, because a big center can become a target.

9
00:00:18,010 --> 00:00:20,434
Black attacks it from the side, with a pawn break

10
00:00:20,434 --> 00:00:22,710
at the right moment and with the bishop on the

11
00:00:22,710 --> 00:00:25,035
long diagonal, which gets stronger every time a

12
00:00:25,035 --> 00:00:26,222
central pawn disappears.

13
00:00:26,222 --> 00:00:28,795
As you watch, keep asking one question: what is that

14
00:00:28,795 --> 00:00:31,318
bishop looking at, and what is standing in its way?

15
00:00:32,068 --> 00:00:33,116
Now the line itself.

16
00:00:33,116 --> 00:00:35,369
White plays the classical setup, with pawns

17
00:00:35,369 --> 00:00:37,465
on e4 and d4, both knights out, a bishop

18
00:00:37,465 --> 00:00:39,037
developed, and a castled king.

19
00:00:39,037 --> 00:00:40,871
Black answers in the same calm way.

20
00:00:40,871 --> 00:00:42,495
After that, the middlegame is a

21
00:00:42,495 --> 00:00:43,910
fight about White's center.

22
00:00:43,910 --> 00:00:46,740
Do not be passive, because White's space can turn into

23
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:48,835
a king-side attack if you wait too long.

24
00:00:48,835 --> 00:00:51,193
Choose a pawn break, either the d-pawn or the

25
00:00:51,193 --> 00:00:52,870
e-pawn, prepare it, and play it.

26
00:00:52,870 --> 00:00:55,752
Once pawns come off, the long-diagonal bishop wakes up.

27
00:00:56,502 --> 00:00:58,151
White opens by grabbing space in the

28
00:00:58,151 --> 00:00:59,433
center with the king's pawn.

29
00:00:59,433 --> 00:01:01,586
It is the most popular first move in chess, and

30
00:01:01,586 --> 00:01:03,143
Black's setup is built to meet it.

31
00:01:03,893 --> 00:01:05,108
Here is the signature move.

32
00:01:05,108 --> 00:01:07,044
The g-pawn steps up one square, which opens

33
00:01:07,044 --> 00:01:08,620
the diagonal for the king's bishop.

34
00:01:08,620 --> 00:01:11,050
It looks odd, but few players at this level have faced

35
00:01:11,050 --> 00:01:13,076
it, so you will know more than your opponent.

36
00:01:13,826 --> 00:01:16,425
White builds the big center, with pawns on d4 and e4.

37
00:01:16,425 --> 00:01:19,023
Do not worry, this is exactly what the setup invites.

38
00:01:19,023 --> 00:01:20,984
Those pawns need defending, and they are

39
00:01:20,984 --> 00:01:22,406
what Black will aim at later.

40
00:01:23,156 --> 00:01:25,695
The bishop lands on the long diagonal, pointing at

41
00:01:25,695 --> 00:01:27,879
the center and the far corner of the board.

42
00:01:27,879 --> 00:01:30,265
Most of Black's play comes from this one piece.

43
00:01:30,265 --> 00:01:33,109
A common slip here is knight to c6, hitting the d4 pawn.

44
00:01:33,109 --> 00:01:36,156
It is not a blunder, but White just plays d5, the knight has

45
00:01:36,156 --> 00:01:38,644
to move again, and White gains space and a tempo.

46
00:01:39,394 --> 00:01:41,040
White develops the queen's knight.

47
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:42,637
It supports the e-pawn and brings

48
00:01:42,637 --> 00:01:43,992
another piece into the game.

49
00:01:44,742 --> 00:01:46,240
A quiet move with a purpose.

50
00:01:46,240 --> 00:01:48,754
The d-pawn steps up one square, which keeps the

51
00:01:48,754 --> 00:01:51,054
position flexible and covers the e5 square.

52
00:01:51,054 --> 00:01:53,888
A common slip is to play the knight to f6 right away.

53
00:01:53,888 --> 00:01:56,188
Then White pushes the e-pawn, the knight is

54
00:01:56,188 --> 00:01:58,221
driven back, and White has more space.

55
00:01:58,971 --> 00:02:00,546
White develops the second knight.

56
00:02:00,546 --> 00:02:02,884
It covers the center and gets ready for castling.

57
00:02:03,634 --> 00:02:05,658
Now the knight comes out and attacks the

58
00:02:05,658 --> 00:02:07,277
e-pawn, so White has to respond.

59
00:02:07,277 --> 00:02:09,301
A tempting but poor idea here is to open

60
00:02:09,301 --> 00:02:10,668
the center at once with e5.

61
00:02:10,668 --> 00:02:12,793
After the trades, the queens come off with

62
00:02:12,793 --> 00:02:14,868
check, Black's king can no longer castle,

63
00:02:14,868 --> 00:02:16,335
and White's pieces are ahead.

64
00:02:17,085 --> 00:02:19,518
White defends the e-pawn by developing the bishop.

65
00:02:19,518 --> 00:02:21,172
It is also a step toward castling.

66
00:02:21,922 --> 00:02:24,005
Black castles, and the king is safe behind

67
00:02:24,005 --> 00:02:25,592
the bishop on the long diagonal.

68
00:02:25,592 --> 00:02:27,774
Again, resist the urge to play e5 right now.

69
00:02:27,774 --> 00:02:30,502
With the pieces not ready, White simply wins that pawn.

70
00:02:31,252 --> 00:02:32,338
White castles as well.

71
00:02:32,338 --> 00:02:34,905
Both kings are safe, development is nearly done, and

72
00:02:34,905 --> 00:02:37,127
the real middlegame battle is about to begin.

73
00:02:37,877 --> 00:02:39,658
And here is the flexible move, c6.

74
00:02:39,658 --> 00:02:42,905
It prepares a central break with the d-pawn or the e-pawn, and

75
00:02:42,905 --> 00:02:45,419
it gives Black room to expand on the queen-side.

76
00:02:45,419 --> 00:02:48,561
From here the plan is yours: choose a break, prepare it, and

77
00:02:48,561 --> 00:02:50,970
strike before White's space becomes an attack.

78
00:02:50,970 --> 00:02:52,908
When the center opens, that bishop on

79
00:02:52,908 --> 00:02:54,479
the long diagonal comes alive.
